\section{A Cell Formula Defines a Dependency, Not a Command}

In a spreadsheet, changing an input cell causes dependent formulas to be recalculated. Finkel observes that the internal representation resembles a dataflow graph, while the grid-oriented programming language differs sharply from an ordered text of commands. Consider this small sheet:

\begin{verbatim}
A2: 12
B2: =A2*1.20
C2: =IF(B2>10, "high", "low")
D2: =SUM(A2:A10)
\end{verbatim}

\texttt{B2} depends on \texttt{A2}; \texttt{C2} depends on \texttt{B2}; \texttt{D2} depends on a range whose membership can change if rows are inserted. Recalculation order may differ as long as pure formulas observe a consistent dependency state. Volatile functions, external data, macros, iterative circular calculation, and errors complicate that claim. A spreadsheet is reactive, but it is not automatically a pure deterministic dataflow language \cite{blackwell2001spreadsheet}.

\section{Make References and Invalidations First-Class}

A spreadsheet IR should give every cell and formula occurrence a stable identity. Formula nodes retain relative, absolute, and mixed references rather than prematurely converting all references to coordinates; copy and insertion semantics depend on those forms. Range nodes, dynamic lookup, volatility, error values, locale-sensitive parsing, spill arrays, and external effects also need explicit representation. The dependency graph is derived from resolved references and may include conservative dynamic edges.

\Cref{fig:visual-370} distinguishes dependency reachability from the smaller dirty set after an edit.

